Chronic Bronchitis
Respiratory System
Productive cough >3 months/2 years, Morning sputum, Blue bloater phenotype

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-010
Reason for Visit: Worsening of chronic morning cough with new-onset peripheral edema and worsening dyspnea, requiring urgent assessment for right heart failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with chronic productive cough now developing central cyanosis, worsened dyspnea, and leg swelling. Urgent after-hours evaluation was necessary to assess for cor pulmonale and acute hypoxemic respiratory failure before the regular in-hours clinic opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've always had my morning cough, but now my legs are swelling up and I can't catch my breath just walking to the bathroom."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a long-standing history of a productive cough occurring for at least three months of the year for two consecutive years. They describe expectoration of thick, mucoid sputum, which is most severe in the morning. Over the last week, progressive exertional dyspnea has worsened. They suffer from frequent mild respiratory infections. The patient notes their lips have looked blue recently, and their shoes no longer fit due to mild peripheral edema.
Functional Status: Usually independent; currently limiting ambulation due to shortness of breath and leg heaviness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Heavy smoking history; reluctance to engage with healthcare systems.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 mmHg | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 22 breaths/min | SpO2: 89% on room air | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Stocky/overweight body habitus ("blue bloater" phenotype). Central cyanosis noted at the lips and mucous membranes.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals a prolonged expiratory phase with scattered wheezing and coarse rhonchi that clear partially with coughing. Cardiovascular exam shows an elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP) and bilateral 2+ pitting edema to the mid-calves, indicating early cor pulmonale. Hypoxemia is present on pulse oximetry.
Environmental Observations: Ashtrays visible; strong odor of cigarette smoke in the residence.
Medication Reconciliation: Non-compliant with prescribed inhaled bronchodilators.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Bronchitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Left-Sided Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fine bibasilar crackles, presence of prominent purulent morning sputum and rhonchi.

Obstructive Sleep Apnea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of significant chronic daytime sputum production and chronic cough, though OSA may be a concurrent comorbidity.

Bronchiectasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sputum is mucoid rather than copious and foul-smelling, absence of coarse localized basilar crackles.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic "blue bloater" phenotype of chronic bronchitis, characterized by a chronic productive cough, stocky build, and central cyanosis due to chronic hypoxemia. The acute presentation of worsening dyspnea, central cyanosis, and new-onset peripheral edema indicates the development of right heart failure (cor pulmonale) secondary to pulmonary hypertension. The unmanaged hypoxemia (SpO2 89%) poses a critical risk. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have permitted further right ventricular strain and potential cardiovascular collapse. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly justified to initiate controlled oxygen therapy, diuretic management for fluid overload, and optimize bronchodilation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered nebulized ipratropium/salbutamol. Initiated careful low-dose oral furosemide (40mg) for peripheral edema. Commenced continuous oxygen via nasal cannula targeted to SpO2 88-92%.
Positive Behavioral Change: Place all cigarette packs and lighters in a locked box in the garage; removing the immediate visual trigger and physical access directly disrupts the automatic smoking habit, reducing the daily toxic load on the lungs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to chronic disease management nurse.
DME/Safety (Optional): Home oxygen concentrator required.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 24 hours to monitor fluid status and arrange formal spirometry to document obstructive pattern (FEV1/FVC < 0.70).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to Emergency Department (call 000) if severe shortness of breath develops at rest, if chest pain occurs, or if the patient becomes unusually drowsy or confused.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of right heart failure requires formal echocardiography; oxygen targets must be strictly adhered to.
Clinical Course: The leg swelling will reduce over several days with the water tablet; the chronic cough will persist but breathing should become easier with regular inhaler use.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you feel confused, if you cannot wake up properly, or if your breathing becomes so difficult you cannot speak.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The long-term lack of oxygen has strained the right side of your heart, causing fluid to back up into your legs; the water tablet removes this fluid, and the oxygen reduces the strain on the heart.
Actionable Steps: Weigh yourself every morning after using the toilet and before breakfast; tracking this specific number allows you to catch fluid build-up early, directly preventing another severe exacerbation and hospitalization.

